  "stem": "A supine chest X-ray in a neonate shows a central venous tube inserted via the neck. Where should the tip of a correctly positioned umbilical venous or central venous catheter lie on chest X-ray in a neonate?",
  "questionType": "SBA",
  "options": [
    {
      "key": "A",
      "text": "In the external jugular vein",
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "key": "B",
      "text": "In the internal jugular vein",
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "key": "C",
      "text": "At the cavoatrial junction or lower right atrium",
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "key": "D",
      "text": "In the left atrium",
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "explanation": "In neonates, the ideal tip position for a central venous catheter is at the cavoatrial junction, and it may project into the lower right atrium depending on the catheter type and clinical context. The external and internal jugular veins are too proximal, and the left atrium is incorrect.",
